\section{Peak diversity is zero below the country}{

In general \code{peak(parent) <= sum(peak(children))}, the gap being load
diversity. Here it is \strong{exactly zero within every country}, and appears only
across Europe (580 GW summed against 543 GW coincident, 6.8\%).

That is a property of the data, not of European demand. PyPSA-Eur builds
per-bus load as a static fraction times the \emph{national} hourly series, so
every bus in a country carries an identical normalised shape. Going finer
than a country adds spatial detail to the \strong{level} of demand and none to its
\strong{shape}: at NUTS3, 1,477 regions share 36 distinct profiles.
}

\section{442 regions carry no demand}{

\code{load.substation_only: true} places demand only on substation buses, and 442
of the 1,477 NUTS3 regions contain none — Germany alone has 182. They are
present with \code{load_twh = 0} and \code{n_buses = 0} rather than omitted, so the gap
shows on a map instead of vanishing. It is a real ceiling on useful
resolution.
}

